That part's right, but you didn't understand the
trick he used after it.  He _wants_ to start at the
beginning of the line, because he wants to look for
the _first_ comma, not just "any old comma", that
way he gets exactly the comma that comes after the
(expanded) $USERNAME, not just some comma after
something that _looks like_ an expanded $USERNAME.

>> Seems easy =]

>> jt,killed by a newt called 1 2 3 Aaa Aaa Aaa Aaa marvin,a {}

>> Really, you need that caret.

> Well, that would match on "marvin"; you're right.

> What _does_ the leading "^[^,]\*" in your example do?

And that's the important extra tool you need to add
to your Perl regex matching capabilities. That much
of his expression says: Start at the beginning of
the line, and eat anything that _isn't_ a comma, up
until...

Then he followed with the space, the expanded
$USERNAME and the comma. By doing it this way, he
_always_ makes the attempt to match the expanded
$USERNAME against _exactly_ the text abutting to the
left of the the _first_ comma from the _start
of the line_!

That way, possible matches against a $USERNAME
elsewhere in the line are completely ignored.

Perl regular expression matching is a splendid tool,
one of the real triumphs of humankind, but using it
without knowing what it does _in detail_ is like
using a table saw without a safety guard: you can
easily get badly hurt.

My own practice is always to have the Camel Book
open to that section when I write regex-s to make
sure I'm both doing them right and not missing some
slick trick to do one better.

xanthian.


In more detail, 

  `/usr/bin/grep \"^[^,]\* $username,[a-z]\" $data_dir/scores.txt`;

says

 ` -- execute
 /usr/bin/grep -- full path to the program to execute, grep
 \" -- escape a double quote, so that $username will resolve itself
       this double quote and the ending one delimit
       the regular expression to grep, but inside it,
       $username is replaced by that enviromental
       variable's (or Perl static variable's) contents
 ^ -- start at the beginning of the line
 [^,] -- select any character which is _not_ a comma
 \* -- escaped asterisk, means, "as many as possible"
       (of those non-commas)
   -- a literal space character, which must match
      after the string of non-commas; I suspect,
      since the space is a delimiter, it should be
      escaped instead with a backslash in front of
      it; doing so is at least safer
 $username -- a variable which expands to the user
	      name, being used in the current loop;
	      might, for example, replace itself
              with "raven"
 , -- a literal comma, which must match after and
      adjacent to the expanded $username value
 [a-z] -- superfluous, we already know we have the
          correct $username, but it would have been
          easier and better to just use [^ ], "any
          character but a space".
 \" -- an escaped double quote to match the opening
       double quote; again, escaped only so that
       $username will expand itself.
 $data_dir/scores.txt -- the input data set as a
			 variable containing the
			 path, followed by a slash
			 delimiter, followed by the
			 literal score table data
                         file name scores.txt.

 ` -- closes the string being passed to the
      system to be executed.

All off the top of my head, my software
